<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Parshvanatha</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Parshva</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Parshvanatha is the 23rd tirthankara of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a> and the earliest Jain teacher widely regarded as a historical figure, traditionally placed about 250 years before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavira">Mahavira</a>. Jain tradition says he was a prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a>, son of King Ashvasena, who renounced the world and taught a fourfold restraint. He is commonly shown sheltered by a canopy of serpent hoods, and Shikharji in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jharkhand">Jharkhand</a> is revered as the place of his liberation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/parshvanatha.jpg" alt="Parshvanatha" width="320" height="367" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fawks3107 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jirawala_Parshwanath.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
